step 2: check the repository is ok or not:(with root)


apt search postgresql-13-pgaudit
 before download,you have to check the dependency for packages:

apt install --download-only -y pkgname
apt install --assum-no  ./postgres-13-pgadut...(write the full name for package)
apt download postgresql-13-pgaudit
apt intsall postgresql-13-pgaudit

when you download the package, the package will be in this place:/etc/apt

step 3:with postgres

backup PostgreSQL.conf and pg_hba.conf 

step 4:with postgres

vim /etc/postgresql/main/postgres.conf

check shared_preload_libraries, if the other package is added,for dependency of packages,the order of the name of package is important:
 add this line  shared_preload_libraries = 'pgaudit',if it exits ,append 'pgaudit' it.

step 5: with root: restart the postgres:

systemctl restart postgres@13-main.service

then check it:
systemctl status postgres@13-main.service

step 6: with user postgres:
login and connect to every database with like :
psql > \c boc

then CREATE EXTENSION pgaudit;
check the extention in every database:
SELECT * FROM pg_extension WHERE extname = 'pgaudit';

step 7 : with user postgres:
per database 
run:

alter system set log_destination = 'csvlog';
alter system set logging_collector = 'on';
alter system set log_filename='postgresql-%Y-%m-%d_%H%M%S.log';
alter system set log_connections = 'on';
alter system set log_rotation_age='7d'; 
alter system set log_rotation_size =0; 
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgaudit.log TO 'ddl,  role'; 

then per database:
select pg_reload_conf();

step 8:
restart the postgres with root:

systemctl restart postgres@13-main.service

then check it:
systemctl status postgres@13-main.service
